A||||6||bumpus23||Autocrossing a Fiero
Z||000000||bumpus23||04-19-99||06:34 PM||||After too much watching, I'm finally getting in on the action, and preparing my Fiero for SCCA Solo2 competition. It's been a bone stock daily driver 86 2M6 SE for the last 120K miles. <p>This car is now going to be used for autocrossing and the trip around a road course. What things should I do (besides learning to drive well, which is a different story) to make this car competetive?<p>Improvements already done or in process include Fiero Store lowering springs, KYB gas shocks, rear anti-roll bar and new (stock) clutch.<p>Improvements I'm considering, and looking for comments on are: Grand Am brake upgrade (front only, I like my parking brake), stiffer front roll bar, poly rear control arm bushings, MSD ignition, relocating the battery, and tires (Hoosier radial slicks, BFG g-force or Yokohama A032R).<p>Also, when it comes to autocrossing, bigger diameter is not necessarily better when it comes to wheels, so I'm thinking of putting the racing tires on a set of factory 14" rims. Is there any reason I shouldn't do this?<p>Can anybody comment on any of these things? 
Z||000001||PaUL||04-19-99||06:41 PM||||hey bumpus,<br>what about changing the camber on the front wheels? did you that? If so tell me how and what effect it had.<br>
Z||000002||theogre||04-19-99||07:23 PM||||consider replacing ball joints and tierod ends. after allot of highway miles, they likely won't last to long racing.<p>urethane bushings Every where, and I do mean everywhere you can get them front, rear, cradle, dogbone, motor mounts, sway bars.......<p>add a rear sway bar, most of your competitors probably have.<p>you could even put in steel, or AL, cradle bushings. and do half a hunred other things, but a complete urethane kit is a very good start.
Z||000003||Motorhead||04-20-99||03:55 PM||||Your'e right Ogre, great start!<p>How 'bout tires though guys! What's the autocrossers choice for this car?<p>Ran Pirrellis' on the old Sunbeam, Yokohama on the Datsun, and whatever I could afford on the MG.  What's "THE" tire for this car?<p>Isn't this the topic of choice 'round the cone laden parking lots?
Z||000004||bumpus23||04-20-99||04:54 PM||||As far as tires go, I recently took a few laps around a road course in a Porsche 944 Turbo with the BFGoodrich g-force race tires, and pulled more than 1G of lateral acceleration with a stock suspension, according to the g-meter. Those things seem to stick like glue. <p>cmore, who posts to this list, uses Hoosier Radial Slicks when autocrossing. I'm going with what I can afford, although leaning towards the Hoosiers, because a Fiero is not a Porsche.<p>As far as control arm bushings, I've heard the poly ones drop in, the metal ones require some welding. Where can one get metal bushings?
Z||000005||theogre||04-20-99||08:07 PM||||metal bushings are all custom work as far as I've seen. Might be better to replace the rear control arm with a tube job. You want teflon inserts in the bushing if you are going to still drive the car regularly. metal to metal need way to much attention unless you want them to were out fast.
Z||000006||Ross||04-21-99||12:44 PM||||Bumpus, Ive read in my fiero news letter about a guy who started to use the G-forces in his race preped GT and couldnt say enough about the diffrence in his track times.They came highly recomended!!But dont drive them on the road for you wont have them long if you do.
